From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Christian Pfeiffer <cpfeiffer@rev-crew.info>
Date: Mon, 23 Nov 2026 02:43:51 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 1/6] Fix x86_64 DHooks detours returning the return address

On x86_64, every function with a dynamic detour returns garbage to its
caller: the low half of a return address instead of its value. All
detours return through the post-hook code that
CHook::CreatePostCallback generates, pre-only ones too, and that code
restored the saved registers first and then called
CHook::GetReturnAddress through RAX, jumping to the result:

    Write_RestoreRegisters(jit, HOOKTYPE_POST);   // RAX = the return value
    jit.mov(rax, func.address);                   // RAX = GetReturnAddress
    jit.call(rax);                                // RAX = the return address
    jit.add(rsp, 8);
    jit.jump(rax);                                // the caller gets RAX

So the caller got the return address in RAX, instead of both the
original value and a post-hook's override; a float return in XMM0 could
be lost to the call as well. The 32-bit code saves the scratch
registers around the same call.

Get the return address before the registers are restored, put it into
the stack slot that the post-hook code reserves at its start
(jit.sub(rsp, 8)), restore the registers, RSP pointing at that slot
again, and return with ret.

To reproduce, on a 64-bit Windows server: detour any function that
returns an int with a pre callback that returns MRES_Ignored, and call
the function with SDKCall before and after enabling the detour. Before,
it returns its value; detoured, a large number. A post callback that
overrides the value with MRES_Override has no effect either.

Tested with Day of Infamy's 64-bit Windows server (srcds_x64):
- Under Wine (wine-cachyos 10.0), DHooks built with clang-cl and xwin's
  MSVC libraries: CINSRules::GetTotalActivePlayersOnTeam(int) returns
  0 for team 3 when called directly; through a pre detour that changes
  nothing, master returns 1408306087, this patch 0, and an override of
  1234 in a post callback arrives with this patch only.
- On Windows 11, built with MSVC 14.52 (Visual Studio 2026), on top of
  the patch of thiscall detours without params, without which any
  thiscall detour with params crashes there: without this patch, the
  pre detour returns -2131523849 and the override doesn't arrive; with
  it, 0 and 1234.

Linux x86_64 has no DHooks yet, but its JIT code is the same apart from
the argument registers, so this would apply there too (the enabler in
alliedmodders/sourcemod#2292 kept this order).
---
 extensions/dhooks/DynamicHooks/hook.cpp | 18 +++++++++++-------
 1 file changed, 11 insertions(+), 7 deletions(-)

diff --git a/extensions/dhooks/DynamicHooks/hook.cpp b/extensions/dhooks/DynamicHooks/hook.cpp
index 55e4f8aa..8dfb8cb3 100644
--- a/extensions/dhooks/DynamicHooks/hook.cpp
+++ b/extensions/dhooks/DynamicHooks/hook.cpp
@@ -407,10 +407,8 @@ void CHook::CreatePostCallback()
 	// Call the post-hook handler
 	Write_CallHandler(jit, HOOKTYPE_POST);
 
-	// Restore the previously saved registers, so any changes will be applied
-	Write_RestoreRegisters(jit, HOOKTYPE_POST);
-
-	// Get return address
+	// Get the return address before restoring the registers. The call clobbers RAX and XMM0,
+	// which hold the (possibly overridden) return value of the function.
 	union
 	{
 		void* (__cdecl CHook::*GetReturnAddress)(void*);
@@ -436,9 +434,15 @@ void CHook::CreatePostCallback()
 	// Free shadow space
 	MSVC_ONLY(jit.add(rsp, 40));
 
-	// Jump to the original return address
-	jit.add(rsp, 8);
-	jit.jump(rax);
+	// Put the original return address into the stack slot reserved above
+	jit.mov(rsp(), rax);
+
+	// Restore the previously saved registers, so any changes will be applied.
+	// This restores RSP to point at that slot again.
+	Write_RestoreRegisters(jit, HOOKTYPE_POST);
+
+	// Return to the original return address
+	jit.retn();
 }
 
 void CHook::Write_CallHandler(x64JitWriter& jit, HookType_t type)
